<div class="prose">

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The four rules</div>
  <ol style="margin-bottom: 0;">
    <li>One notebook for the whole course, all year. Not one per unit.</li>
    <li>Every entry opens with the same header line: date, block, entry title.</li>
    <li>The site gives you the entry title. You never invent one.</li>
    <li>The index at the top stays current, because Docs builds it for you.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<p>A notebook that fragments cannot be searched. That is the whole reason for the rules: in April you will want something you wrote in September, and you will find it in about five seconds if the entry titles are consistent and about five minutes if they are not.</p>

<h2>1. Set it up</h2>

<p>Ten minutes in <a href="/units/unit-0/block-1/">Block 1</a>. You do this once.</p>

<ol>
  <li>Create a Google Doc named <strong>Class Notebook - [your name] - E&amp;ES</strong>.</li>
  <li>Put it in your class Drive folder.</li>
  <li>Share it with Mr. Ignash, edit access, once. That is the only time you hand anything over. There is no submitting after this.</li>
  <li>Turn on <strong>View, Show outline</strong>. Leave it on. It is how you move around a document that will run to a hundred entries.</li>
</ol>

<p>Sharing once means the notebook is checked, not collected. It is not graded, and you never lose access to it while I look at it. It also means I can see it at any time, so write it as work you are willing to have read.</p>

<h2>2. The header line</h2>

<p>Every entry opens with one line, in this order: date, block, entry title.</p>

<div class="entry-header-demo">A: Aug 14 | U0 B3 | AI as a Source + Documentation Protocol</div>

<p><strong>Style that line as Heading 2.</strong> That is not decoration. Docs builds the index out of your Heading 2s, so a header line styled as normal text is an entry that never appears in your index and that you will not find again.</p>

<p>A and B rotations meet on different days, so every block page shows you both dates. Take yours.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">You never invent an entry title</div>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">The Do section of every block page prints the exact header line for that day. Copy it. This is what makes the whole system work: every notebook in the room says the same thing in the same order, so mine takes ten seconds to check and yours is searchable by a phrase you did not have to remember inventing.</p>
</div>

<h2>3. The index</h2>

<p>At the very top of the doc, above your first entry: <strong>Insert, Table of contents</strong>, and pick the version <strong>with links</strong>.</p>

<p>That is the whole job. It updates itself from your Heading 2s, so there is no column to rule and no page number to write. If an entry is missing from it, the header line was not styled as Heading 2.</p>

<h2>4. Working in it</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Never delete an entry.</strong> If something is wrong, write the correction underneath it. The record of you changing your mind is worth more than a tidy page, and in this course you will be asked to show that change more than once.</li>
  <li><strong>Bell ringer goes under the same header.</strong> One block, one entry. Your bell ringer, your notes, and your sketches all sit under today's header line.</li>
  <li><strong>Working offline.</strong> Turn on offline access for the doc (in Drive, the three-dot menu, Available offline). If the wifi drops mid-lesson you keep typing and it syncs later. A dropped connection should not cost you an entry.</li>
</ul>

<h2>5. If you take notes by ear</h2>

<p>You can record instead of typing. The rule is narrow: <strong>the header line is always typed, as Heading 2. The body underneath it can be a recording.</strong></p>

<ol>
  <li>Record into the <strong>Notebook audio</strong> subfolder in your class Drive folder.</li>
  <li>Type the header line in the doc as usual.</li>
  <li>Paste the link to the recording underneath it.</li>
</ol>

<p>Doing it this way keeps you in the index exactly like everyone else. An audio entry that never gets a typed header is an entry you cannot find, which is the one outcome this whole page exists to prevent.</p>

<h2>6. What goes somewhere else</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>End-of-block reflections</strong> go in the Reflection Journal tool, which you reach from Google Classroom. Not here. See <a href="/foundations/how-this-class-works/">How This Class Works</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Graded work</strong> goes to Google Classroom.</li>
  <li><strong>The Unit 3 engineering journal</strong> is its own separate artifact with its own format. It is not part of this notebook.</li>
</ul>

<p>What is left, and what this notebook is for: your bell ringers, your <a href="/foundations/notetaking/#power-notes">Power Notes</a>, and your <a href="/foundations/notetaking/#sketch-to-stretch">sketches</a>. How to make those notes worth keeping is a different question, and it is answered at <a href="/foundations/notetaking/">Notetaking &amp; Notemaking</a>.</p>

<h2>7. If you join partway through the year</h2>

<p>Start at the block you arrive on. Set the doc up the same way and copy the header lines from that day forward. You do not need to backfill and you are not behind.</p>

